{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And it you shall not cut down, for the man is a tree of the field.</b> The commentators, of blessed memory, explained that the life of man and his sustenance is the tree of the field, in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 24:18) ״כי נפש הוא חובל״, and therefore I command you not to destroy it, for there is blessing in it. And in my opinion, the word האדם is drawn toward לא תכרות, and the meaning of the verse is: for the man is not a tree of the field, that he should come before you in a siege like the man — and it is not the way of a wise and understanding people to destroy a thing fit for use for no purpose. Therefore you should not expend effort to cut down the tree of the field, but rather you should guard against destroying it and damaging it, and take from it its benefit — this is ״כי ממנו תאכל״. And if you destroy it, you are found to be one who damages and causes the loss of benefit. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: ״כי ממנו תאכל״ — if you saw a worthy Torah scholar, 'from it you may eat and it you shall not cut down'; and if not, 'it you shall destroy and cut down.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:49:43.925666028Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}